<p class="lt-chem-21788">Instead, some of the ions exist as ion pairs, a cation and an anion that for a brief time are associated with each other without an intervening shell of water molecules (Figure \(\PageIndex{9}\)). Each of these temporary units behaves like a single dissolved particle until it dissociates. Highly charged ions such as \(Mg^{2+}\), \(Al^{3+}\), \(SO_4^{2&minus;}\), and \(PO_4^{3&minus;}\) have a greater tendency to form ion pairs because of their strong electrostatic interactions. The actual number of solvated ions present in a solution can be determined by measuring a colligative property at several solute concentrations.</p>

