<div class="box-example">
<span id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B8.7D.5C):_Iron_Chloride_in_Water"></span><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map:_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/13:_Properties_of_Solutions/13.05:_Colligative_Properties" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B8.7D.5C):_Iron_Chloride_in_Water"></span><h5 class="box-legend" id="Example_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B8.7D.5C):_Iron_Chloride_in_Water-21788"><span class="lt-icon-default">Example \(\PageIndex{8}\): Iron Chloride in Water</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-21788">A 0.0500 M aqueous solution of \(FeCl_3\) has an osmotic pressure of 4.15 atm at 25&deg;C. Calculate the van&rsquo;t Hoff factor \(i\) for the solution.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788"><strong>Given</strong>: solute concentration, osmotic pressure, and temperature</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788"><strong>Asked for</strong>: van&rsquo;t Hoff factor</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788"><strong>Strategy</strong>:</p>

<ol start="1" style="list-style-type: upper-alpha;">
    <li class="lt-chem-21788">Use Equation \(\ref{13.5.12}\) to calculate the expected osmotic pressure of the solution based on the effective concentration of dissolved particles in the solvent.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-21788">Calculate the ratio of the observed osmotic pressure to the expected value. Multiply this number by the number of ions of solute per formula unit, and then use Equation \(\ref{13.5.13}\) to calculate the van&rsquo;t Hoff factor.</li>
</ol>

<p class="lt-chem-21788"><strong>Solution</strong>:</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788">A If \(\ce{FeCl_3}\) dissociated completely in aqueous solution, it would produce four ions per formula unit [Fe3+(aq) plus 3Cl&minus;(aq)] for an effective concentration of dissolved particles of 4 &times; 0.0500 M = 0.200 M. The osmotic pressure would be</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-21788">\[\begin{align*} \Pi &amp;=MRT \\[4pt] &amp;=(0.200 \;mol/L) \left[0.0821\;(L&sdot;atm)/(K&sdot;mol) \right] (298\; K)=4.89\; atm \end{align*} \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788">B The observed osmotic pressure is only 4.15 atm, presumably due to ion pair formation. The ratio of the observed osmotic pressure to the calculated value is 4.15 atm/4.89 atm = 0.849, which indicates that the solution contains (0.849)(4) = 3.40 particles per mole of \(\ce{FeCl_3}\) dissolved. Alternatively, we can calculate the observed particle concentration from the osmotic pressure of 4.15 atm:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-21788">\[4.15\; atm=M \left( \dfrac{0.0821 \;(L&sdot;atm)}{(K&sdot;mol)}\right) (298 \;K) \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788">or after rearranging</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-21788">\[M = 0.170 mol \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-21788">The ratio of this value to the expected value of 0.200 M is 0.170 M/0.200 M = 0.850, which again gives us (0.850)(4) = 3.40 particles per mole of \(\ce{FeCl_3}\) dissolved. From Equation \(\ref{13.5.13}\), the van&rsquo;t Hoff factor for the solution is</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-21788">\[i=\dfrac{\text{3.40 particles observed}}{\text{1 formula unit}\; \ce{FeCl_3}}=3.40 \nonumber \]</p>
</div></div>

<div class="box-exercise">
